I'll add some performance profiling later tonight just to make sure performance hasn't regressed, but the main goal here was the above memory improvements.

There's a couple of things of note:
- Panels are currently loaded when they come into the visible region of the carousel. We optimally want them to load even when they become visible in the "bleed" areas. This will require some changes to the framework in `DelayedLoadContainer` to allow more control over the intersection function.
- There's a lot of allocation churn due to the `DelayedLoadContainer` constructions. I see this as being something we can build towards improving. A likely method forward will be to *not* remake the `DelayedLoadContainer` and make the components beneath it support asynchronous updating (easily said for the background, but text also needs to be asynchronous as it may be loading new unicode characters).
- There is inherently a bit more coupling than before between the `DrawableCarousel` classes and the `BeatmapCarousel` itself, but as this is quite a closed system I don't see this as being too bad.
- The benchmark above was tested with 5,000 panels, but I have tested 50,000 and 500,000 outside of profiling (this can be done by adjusting the `const` in the test scene). Scroll performance is linear; select performance becomes noticeably higher but the cause it outside the scope of this PR.
